Build **Arc Foundry**, an electro-industrial tower-defense game that runs entirely in
the browser, and **produce its art, effects, and audio yourself**, during this build,
with the asset-generation tools on your `PATH`.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that pins
**Playwright** as a dev dependency and depends on the
**`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`** library (below). Its dependencies have already
been installed for you: `node_modules/` is populated and Playwright's headless
**Chromium** is downloaded, so you can build right away (see "Playwright and Chromium"
below for what they are there for). Build on this project rather than replacing it:
keep the `package.json`, add your framework, bundler, and a `build` script on top of
it, and keep the lockfile in sync (see below).

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly. It is decomposed by concern:
`specs/overview.md` (the pitch, goals, hard requirements, the stage, the palette and
type, and the visual design), `specs/board.md` (the tile grid, the uniform component
footprint, the waypoint pathing and mazing model, and the three maps),
`specs/enemies.md` (the Load roster, the flyer, the Dynamo boss, and the per-wave HP
scaling), `specs/towers.md` (the eight component types, the quality ladder, the stat
tables, the combination towers, and the rotating-head and traveling-projectile
rules), `specs/build.md` (the scrap-press build loop and the UPGRADE QUALITY track),
`specs/gameplay.md` (the economy, Grid Integrity, the wave campaign, and the
maze-rating finale), `specs/ui.md` (the game states, the required menus, and the HUD),
`specs/modes.md` (the in-game difficulty and the
map- and difficulty-select menus), `specs/controls.md`, `specs/assets.md` (the
asset-production contract), `specs/instrumentation.md` (the debugging and automation
API), and `specs/proof.md`.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics, no backend or credentials, the palette and type, a
`README.md`, the game states, and the `window.__foundry` debugging and automation API
and debug overlay (`specs/instrumentation.md`) — are stated in full in
`specs/overview.md` (and the files it points to). That specification is
authoritative; build to it rather than to a summary here. Three things bear
restating, because the served build and the produced assets depend on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided `package.json`
  at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run `npm install` after you
  change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci` then `npm run build` must
  produce the static site, with no other manual step, into one of `dist/`, `build/`,
  or `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory.
  Serving that directory as-is must run the game, and it must work **at any base path**
  (it is played back from a per-run sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at
  runtime must be page-relative, never a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative
  base such as `base: './'`). `specs/overview.md` and `specs/assets.md` state this in
  full; follow it exactly.
- **You PRODUCE the art, effects, and audio.** There is **no** pre-made game art in
  this project. Instead, the run image puts six asset-generation tools on your
  `PATH` — **`draw`** and **`draw-sheet`** (sprites and animated sprite sheets),
  **`particle-2d`** (particle systems), and **`sfx-synth`**, **`sfx-sample`**, and
  **`music`** (sound and music) — and you must **produce every asset the game plays
  with these tools during this build**, then wire the produced files into the game.
  Run each tool's `--help` to learn its operations. **`specs/assets.md` is the
  authoritative contract**: it enumerates every sprite, animation, particle system,
  and sound the game needs, names which tool produces each and where it lands under
  `assets/`, and states how each is wired into the build, including that the
  **electrical effects** (the arcs, chain-lightning, spark showers, and discharges
  that are the headline of this build) are `particle-2d` systems played live through
  the **`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`** library already installed as a dependency
  of your project (import it like any other dependency; do not fetch or reimplement
  it). Load every produced file with page-relative URLs so it resolves from the served
  sub-path.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are **illustrative examples, not targets to reproduce**, of the
  title, map-select, gameplay, and Overload screens. Design your own menus and layout
  from the specification; the images only illustrate one way the intended look can come
  together.

## Playwright and Chromium

The project's **Playwright** and its headless **Chromium** are installed, and the
container has network access, so you can drive the built game in a real browser. Using
them to validate your build as you go — serving it, driving a wave, exercising the
`window.__foundry` API — is a good way to catch problems, but whether and how far to do
so is your call. (You will separately need them for the proof-of-implementation
captures that `specs/proof.md` asks for.)

Whenever you do launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it from
your project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH`;
it lives in your project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium
with the `--no-sandbox` argument: this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is **not**
published. When the build is complete, commit everything: run `git add -A` then `git
commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing
uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/` and the
build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does not mean committing those,
but it does mean committing your source, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`,
the **produced assets under `assets/`**, and the `proof/` artifacts.
